\chapter{A worked project from source to kernel-backed execution}
\label{ch:118}
\index{A worked project from source to kernel-backed execution}

The standard-library extension program is an end-to-end exercise that uses source imports, ordinary checked wrappers, generated C, native bridge groups and MuLIB providers. Begin with dependency inspection to find the selected source modules. Then build and run the example via the same compiler and search roots. This tests the conventional bridge path described in \cref{ch:98}.

\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
on1xc --deps on1x/examples/stdlib_extensions.on1x
python3 on1x/tools/on1x-build.py \
  on1x/examples/stdlib_extensions.on1x --on1xc "$(command -v on1xc)" \
  -o build/stdlib-example
build/stdlib-example
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

Select one pure helper and one bridge-backed call. Trace each through its source library and compare the plan needed to execute it. Add a missing-field fixture to the format operation and check both its status and payload. Split this demonstration into a small project with directory-local declarations once it runs as a single source entry. The same analysis applies when replacing one native companion with a pinned archive.

\section{Predict the transcript before building}
The example asks for the value of workers in the app section of a
short INI document. Success gives status zero and text containing
the two digits 42. This is still text: the pure parser consumes it
from position zero and reports three things, a Boolean, a signed
integer and an ending position. The position is two. Comparing it
with the text length matters because a successful prefix parse need
not consume an entire configuration value. Only after both checks
does the program print the number.

It then passes deliberately invalid text to JSON normalization. The
source wrapper takes the native result and pairs it with the bridge
status. The example asserts status minus two and prints a line about
the recoverable failure. Thus the expected stdout is:
\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
42
recoverable parse failure
\end{OnxCodeBlock}
This second line is evidence that control returned to the program
after the format error. A process that simply exited on bad JSON
could pass a test for the first line alone.

Make two small edits in separate copies. With workers set to
\OnxCodeTerm{+42!}, the parser recognizes the signed prefix and stops
at position three, before the exclamation mark. The program prints
``invalid worker count'' and returns without reaching JSON. With the
requested key absent altogether, the INI wrapper reports missing and
the program prints ``configuration unavailable'' without attempting
integer parsing. Neither change requires a different native arithmetic
kernel: the choice of branch follows the status from lookup and the
cursor returned by the pure source parser.

